```
public fun liquidation_ltv(self: &DeleverageParams, secs_since_activation: u64): Decimal { 
    let hours = secs_since_activation / SECONDS_IN_AN_HOUR;    
    let reduction = self.liquidation_factor_hourly_drop.mul_u64(hours);
    self.liquidation_factor_base.saturating_sub(reduction)
}
```

The liquidation LTV starts at `liquidation_factor_base` and decreases over time by `liquidation_factor_hourly_drop` per hour. 

With `saturating_sub`, the threshold eventually floors at 0, which means after enough time passes, any position with debt becomes liquidatable — the ADL mechanism escalates over time to force deleverage when the protocol needs it.

For normal liquidation, the shortfall check properly enforces that borrowers must be underwater. But ADL uses a custom, time-decaying LTV threshold that can drop below the standard liquidation threshold and reach zero, allowing the protocol to liquidate even healthy borrowers when system debt exceeds limits and the timelock expires. This is intentional design — ADL is the emergency deleveraging tool, not a vulnerability.

